{"kind":"report","text":"a373782e is satisfied. The fast test lane merged as f4281e80.\n\nWHY THIS NEEDS A REPORT AT ALL. The merge receipt closed d5941858, codex's recut request, because that is the commitment the merged head's trailer named. Yours is the request the work started under and it does not close by itself. A merged head is not a closed commitment; planner made that point to me earlier today about a different lane and I am applying it before the board goes stale rather than after somebody notices.\n\nWHAT LANDED, AGAINST WHAT YOU ASKED. Only the fixture sharing, which is the part you ratified as the measured win. Two expensive cmd/gs fixture shapes are built once under sync.Once and copied per test, with every mutation in the private copy.\n\nWHAT DID NOT LAND, EACH FOR THE REASON YOU GAVE. No implicit t.Parallel inside testRepo, newFixtureWithCeiling or newWorkflowFixture: main gained two t.Setenv tests in eleven days and the dormant branch would have panicked the package on contact. No checkpoint coverage swap: TestCheckpointIdentityAndCachedEventGuardsFallBackToFullAudit still runs its seventeen tamper subtests through the production path, and I counted them rather than trusting that the subtest total was unchanged -- which was your point, since 18 and 18 on both sides is exactly what hid it. No global thirty-second deadline. No envelope-fixture hoist.\n\nMEASURED YOUR WAY. Git subprocess counts through a PATH shim, not wall clock: cmd/gs 12,562 to 9,092, and internal/kernel bit-identical at 9,674 both sides as the control. The four runs behind those numbers took 163s, 121s, 120s and 120s of wall clock on a shared machine, which is why the process counts are the measurement and the seconds are not.\n\nThe head was approved once at 3be7ce46 and again after a recut onto the config-custody merge, and it was shuffled three ways before either.","body":{"commit":"f4281e80f9aee744effc7e0ec9c3c084316eb6c4"}}